---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { CONTACT, COMPANY_EMAIL } from "../links";
---
<Base title="Privacy" description="Your files and searches never leave your PC. whereti asks for updates once a day at most, and this website sets no cookies. The details." prose>
  <h1>Privacy</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Your files, their names and your searches never leave your PC, and this website keeps no record of you. Below is
    every request whereti can make, and exactly what it carries.
  </p>
  <p class="updated">Last changed on 26 October 2026: past searches, network folders and search by meaning.</p>

  <h2 id="app">The app</h2>
  <p>
    There is no account, no telemetry and no crash reporting. The document reader and the installer make no network
    requests at all. The search window makes at most two, both about updates, and the background service at most two,
    each once and only if you ask for it:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The update check, at most once a day.</strong> whereti asks
      <code>https://releases.whereti.com/windows/x86_64/0.1.0</code> (its own architecture and version) whether a newer
      version exists. Nothing else is sent: no identifier, no cookie, nothing about your files. The time of each check is
      saved before it goes out, so restarting the PC or the app never makes a second one that day. The server records one
      line per check: the platform, the version that asked, and whether an update was offered. No IP address. Its code is
      public in the <a href="https://github.com/whereti/releases">whereti.releases</a> repository. You can turn the check
      off in Settings, General; then whereti sends nothing at all.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The update download, only when you choose Install update.</strong> The installer comes from whereti's
      GitHub releases, so GitHub sees that download like any other. whereti installs it only if it is signed by Software
      Hive S.R.L. and matches the checksum of the release.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The photo model, once, only if you turn on recognizing objects and animals in photos.</strong> The
      background service downloads CLIP's image model (84 MB) from
      <code>huggingface.co/Xenova/clip-vit-base-patch32</code>, at a fixed revision, and uses it only if its size and
      SHA-256 checksum match. Hugging Face sees that download like any other; nothing about you or your photos is sent.
      The photos are then read on your PC, in the same isolated reader as documents.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The meaning model, once, only if you turn on search by meaning.</strong> The background service downloads
      a small multilingual model and its word list (135 MB in all) from
      <code>huggingface.co/Xenova/multilingual-e5-small</code>, at a fixed revision, and uses them only if their sizes
      and SHA-256 checksums match. Hugging Face sees that download like any other; nothing about you or your documents
      is sent. Documents are then read on your PC, in the same isolated reader.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>Network folders.</strong> If you add a network folder or a mapped drive in Settings, Search, the search window
    reads its file names once an hour, as you, straight from that NAS or server. That stays between your PC and your own
    network.
  </p>
  <p>What whereti keeps, all on your PC:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The index</strong> of file names and document words, in <code>%ProgramData%\whereti</code>. Windows lets
      only itself and administrators read it, because it lists every file on the PC. With search by meaning on, the
      meaning of each document, kept as numbers, sits beside it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your settings</strong> (language, theme, shortcut, folders to skip) in
      <code>%AppData%\whereti\settings.txt</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your past searches</strong>, so <kbd>↑</kbd> can bring them back, in
      <code>%AppData%\whereti\searches.txt</code>. The × next to one forgets it; “Remember your searches” in Settings,
      Search turns this off, and Forget clears the list.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Network folders</strong> you added, in <code>%AppData%\whereti\network.txt</code>, and the names found in them, in a cache in <code>%LocalAppData%\whereti</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>What you open</strong>, so those files come first: the files you opened from whereti and how often, in
      <code>%AppData%\whereti\usage.txt</code>. whereti also reads Windows' own list of recent files. “Forget” in
      Settings, Search clears it; the switch next to it turns it off.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Where you work</strong>, so those folders come first: the folders where your own files were created or
      changed lately, with a score that halves every week, beside the index. Settings, Search shows them; turning it off
      forgets them.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Where photos were taken and what they show</strong>, as words in the index. Places are named from a list
      built into whereti (GeoNames, CC BY 4.0); nothing is looked up online.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Text in scans and pictures is read by the text recognition built into Windows, on your PC; nothing is sent anywhere
    for it. Thumbnails in the Images grid come from Windows' own thumbnail cache. The recent files whereti shows before you type
    come from Windows' own list of recent files. Uninstalling whereti removes the index and the settings.
  </p>

  <h2 id="website">This website</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>No cookies, no analytics, no tracking pixels, no ads.</li>
    <li>Nothing is loaded from another site: the font, the icons and the images are served from whereti.com.</li>
    <li>The server keeps no access log, so ordinary visits leave no IP address behind.</li>
    <li>
      The theme you pick (light or dark) is remembered in your own browser, not sent to us. Choosing the theme of your
      system forgets it.
    </li>
    <li>
      Downloads come from GitHub, which sees the request like any download. GitHub's own privacy statement applies to it.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="supporter">If you buy a supporter license</h2>
  <p>
    Lemon Squeezy handles the payment and keeps what a seller must keep (name, e-mail, country, the invoice), under
    its own privacy policy. It tells our license service the order number, your name and your e-mail, so the key can be
    made and sent. That service keeps only the order number, the key and a one-way hash of your e-mail (to resend a lost
    key), and sends one e-mail with the key, through <a href="https://resend.com/legal/privacy-policy">Resend</a>, which
    delivers it and keeps a delivery log (address, subject, whether it arrived) under its own privacy policy. Your name is
    inside the key, and whereti shows it on your PC only.
  </p>

  <h2 id="email">If you write to us</h2>
  <p>
    E-mails are read by the people who make whereti and kept only as long as the conversation needs. They are not shared
    or used for anything else.
  </p>

  <h2 id="rights">Your rights, formally</h2>
  <p>
    The controller is Software Hive S.R.L., CUI RO54366095, Voicești, Vâlcea, Romania
    (<a href={`mailto:${COMPANY_EMAIL}`}>{COMPANY_EMAIL}</a>). Under the GDPR you can ask what we hold about you, have it
    corrected or deleted, and object to how it is used. In practice we hold nothing unless you have e-mailed us. You can
    also complain to the Romanian data protection authority,
    <a href="https://www.dataprotection.ro">ANSPDCP</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="contact">Contact</h2>
  <p>Questions about privacy: <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT}`}>{CONTACT}</a>.</p>
</Base>
